### Comprehensive Study: Mouse and Keyboard Event Handling with Adapter Classes

#### 1. Why Adapter Classes are Necessary:
Listener interfaces with multiple methods impose high boilerplate overhead:
- Implementing `MouseListener` requires writing empty stubs for 5 methods.
- Implementing `KeyListener` requires stubs for 3 methods (`keyPressed`, `keyReleased`, `keyTyped`).
Adapter classes provide empty implementations so that anonymous classes or custom subclasses need to implement only the methods relevant to the application.

#### 2. Comparison Table: Listener Interfaces vs. Adapter Classes:
| Feature | Event Listener Interface | Event Adapter Class |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Type** | Java `interface` | Abstract `class` |
| **Method Implementation** | Must implement **all** declared methods. | Override **only the required** methods. |
| **Multiple Inheritance** | A class can implement multiple listener interfaces. | A class can extend only **one** adapter class (single inheritance). |
| **Standard Examples** | `MouseListener`, `KeyListener`, `WindowListener` | `MouseAdapter`, `KeyAdapter`, `WindowAdapter` |

#### 3. Complete Demonstrative Java Implementation: Mouse & Keyboard Handling:
```java
import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;
import java.awt.event.*;

public class MouseAndKeyboardEventsDemo extends JFrame {
    private JLabel statusLabel;

    public MouseAndKeyboardEventsDemo() {
        setTitle("Mouse & Keyboard Event Handling Demo");
        setSize(500, 350);
        setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
        setLayout(new BorderLayout());

        statusLabel = new JLabel("Interact with the canvas using Mouse or Keyboard", SwingConstants.CENTER);
        statusLabel.setFont(new Font("Arial", Font.BOLD, 14));
        add(statusLabel, BorderLayout.SOUTH);

        JPanel canvas = new JPanel();
        canvas.setBackground(Color.WHITE);
        canvas.setFocusable(true); // Must be focusable to receive Keyboard events

        // 1. Mouse Event Handling using MouseAdapter
        canvas.addMouseListener(new MouseAdapter() {
            @Override
            public void mouseClicked(MouseEvent e) {
                statusLabel.setText(String.format("Mouse Clicked at Coordinates: X=%d, Y=%d (Button: %d)", 
                                                  e.getX(), e.getY(), e.getButton()));
                canvas.requestFocusInWindow();
            }
        });

        canvas.addMouseMotionListener(new MouseMotionAdapter() {
            @Override
            public void mouseMoved(MouseEvent e) {
                // Tracking mouse hover motion
                statusLabel.setText(String.format("Mouse Hover: X=%d, Y=%d", e.getX(), e.getY()));
            }
        });

        // 2. Keyboard Event Handling using KeyAdapter
        canvas.addKeyListener(new KeyAdapter() {
            @Override
            public void keyPressed(KeyEvent e) {
                statusLabel.setText(String.format("Key Pressed: '%c' [Key Code: %d]", 
                                                  e.getKeyChar(), e.getKeyCode()));
            }
        });

        add(canvas, BorderLayout.CENTER);
        setLocationRelativeTo(null);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SwingUtilities.invokeLater(() -> new MouseAndKeyboardEventsDemo().setVisible(true));
    }
}
```

$$\therefore \text{Adapter classes streamline interactive event processing by eliminating empty interface method stubs.}$$
